Let $f(x)=-\frac{(x+1)^2}{\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{-x^{2}-2x-1}{x^{1/2}}\\
&=-x^{\frac{3}{2}}-2x^{\frac{1}{2}}-x^{-\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply the power rule:

$$
f'(x)=-\frac{3}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}}-x^{-\frac{1}{2}}+\frac{1}{2}x^{-\frac{3}{2}}
$$

The tangent slope at $\left(4,-\frac{25}{2}\right)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=f'(4)&=-\frac{3}{2}\left(4\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}-\left(4\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}+\frac{1}{2}\left(4\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\\
&=-3-\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{16}\\
&=-\frac{55}{16}
\end{aligned}
$$

The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m'&=-\frac{1}{m}\\
&=-\frac{1}{\left(-\frac{55}{16}\right)}\\
&=\frac{16}{55}
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $f(x)=\frac{(x-3)^2}{\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{x^{2}-6x+9}{x^{1/2}}\\
&=x^{\frac{3}{2}}-6x^{\frac{1}{2}}+9x^{-\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply the power rule:

$$
f'(x)=\frac{3}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}}-3x^{-\frac{1}{2}}-\frac{9}{2}x^{-\frac{3}{2}}
$$

The tangent slope at $\left(9,12\right)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=f'(9)&=\frac{3}{2}\left(9\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}-3\left(9\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}-\frac{9}{2}\left(9\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\\
&=\frac{9}{2}-1-\frac{1}{6}\\
&=\frac{10}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m'&=-\frac{1}{m}\\
&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{10}{3}\right)}\\
&=-\frac{3}{10}
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $f(x)=\frac{(x-2)^2}{\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{x^{2}-4x+4}{x^{1/2}}\\
&=x^{\frac{3}{2}}-4x^{\frac{1}{2}}+4x^{-\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply the power rule:

$$
f'(x)=\frac{3}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}}-2x^{-\frac{1}{2}}-2x^{-\frac{3}{2}}
$$

The tangent slope at $\left(4,2\right)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=f'(4)&=\frac{3}{2}\left(4\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}-2\left(4\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}-2\left(4\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\\
&=3-1-\frac{1}{4}\\
&=\frac{7}{4}
\end{aligned}
$$

The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m'&=-\frac{1}{m}\\
&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{7}{4}\right)}\\
&=-\frac{4}{7}
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $f(x)=\frac{(x+2)^2}{\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{x^{2}+4x+4}{x^{1/2}}\\
&=x^{\frac{3}{2}}+4x^{\frac{1}{2}}+4x^{-\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply the power rule:

$$
f'(x)=\frac{3}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}}+2x^{-\frac{1}{2}}-2x^{-\frac{3}{2}}
$$

The tangent slope at $\left(9,\frac{121}{3}\right)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=f'(9)&=\frac{3}{2}\left(9\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}+2\left(9\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}-2\left(9\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\\
&=\frac{9}{2}+\frac{2}{3}-\frac{2}{27}\\
&=\frac{275}{54}
\end{aligned}
$$

The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m'&=-\frac{1}{m}\\
&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{275}{54}\right)}\\
&=-\frac{54}{275}
\end{aligned}
$$
